Why I’d use this structure:

pages should describe app screens, not data logic.
components should render data they are given.
services isolate Alchemy SDK usage so you can change queries without rewriting UI.
hooks keep async logic reusable and prevent repeated loading/error/data code in every page.
utils avoid duplicated formatting logic across the app.
Component Explanations

PageLayout

Wraps the app shell: header, navigation, page container.
Reason: layout should be consistent across all routes, and you do not want each page rebuilding the same shell.
SearchBar

Lets the user enter a block number, tx hash, or address.
Reason: search is central to block explorers. It should be reusable and route users to the correct page.
StatCard

Displays one key value like block number, gas used, balance, timestamp.
Reason: blockchain data is naturally key-value heavy, so one generic display card reduces repeated UI markup.
BlockList

Renders a list of recent blocks.
Reason: the home page needs a quick drill-down entry point.
BlockRow

One block item in the list.
Reason: separating row from list makes the list easier to read and later customize.
TransactionList

Renders transactions for a selected block or address.
Reason: transactions appear in more than one place, so this should not be hardcoded into only the block page.
TransactionRow

One transaction preview row.
Reason: same reason as BlockRow; keeps rendering isolated and reusable.
AddressSummary

Shows normalized address info, maybe ENS if available, and quick links.
Reason: account pages need a clear header summary before showing balance or transfer history.
BalanceCard

Shows ETH balance and maybe nonce/transaction count.
Reason: balance is a distinct concept and deserves a clean dedicated component.
TransfersList

Displays incoming/outgoing transfers for an address.
Reason: transfer history is separate from raw transactions and often easier for users to understand.
NftLookupForm

Collects contract address and token id.
Reason: NFT lookups need input controls and validation, which should be kept separate from display.
NftMetadataCard

Shows image, name, description, collection, token type, attributes.
Reason: NFT metadata is structurally different from block/tx/account data.
LoadingState

Shared loading spinner or skeleton.
Reason: every page will load async blockchain data.
ErrorState

Shared error display.
Reason: RPC/API failures are common enough that error handling should be consistent.
Service Layer Explanations

alchemyClient.js

Creates and exports the configured Alchemy instance.
Reason: there should be one place where network and API key configuration live.
blockService.js

Functions like:
getLatestBlockNumber
getBlockByNumber
getBlockWithTransactions
maybe getRecentBlocks
Reason: block-related logic belongs together and should not leak into pages.
transactionService.js

Functions like:
getTransaction
getTransactionReceipt
getMinedStatus
Reason: transaction details often need multiple SDK calls, and that composition belongs in a service.
accountService.js

Functions like:
getBalance
getTransactionCount
Reason: account lookups are distinct from transaction detail lookups.
transferService.js

Functions like:
getTransfersForAddress
filtering received transfers for a date range
Reason: transfers are one of your learning goals and deserve their own focused module.
nftService.js

Functions like:
getNftMetadata
optional getNftFloorPrice
Reason: NFT methods are a separate Alchemy API area, so isolating them keeps the app easy to reason about.
Why use services instead of calling SDK methods directly in components:

UI becomes easier to read.
Testing and debugging become easier.
If Alchemy response shapes are awkward, you normalize them once in the service instead of everywhere.
Hook Explanations

useRecentBlocks

Fetches and stores latest blocks for home page.
Reason: async fetching plus loading/error state is repetitive; a hook centralizes that behavior.
useBlockDetails

Loads block metadata and transactions for a block page.
Reason: a block page should focus on rendering, not the mechanics of fetching and retrying.
useTransactionDetails

Combines transaction info and receipt info.
Reason: tx details often require more than one request.
useAccountDetails

Loads balance and account-level info.
Reason: keeps account page clean and focused.
useAddressTransfers

Loads transfers filtered by address and possibly date range.
Reason: transfer queries may evolve, and the page should not carry all that logic itself.
useNftMetadata

Loads NFT data based on contract and token id.
Reason: form input and fetch state are easier to manage in a dedicated hook.
Why use hooks here:

In React, hooks are the natural place for reusable async state logic.
They avoid copy-pasting useEffect, useState, loading, and error handling across pages.
They are simpler than introducing a full state library for a bootcamp project.
Why I am not recommending heavier patterns

I would avoid:

Redux
React Query at the start
a backend server right now
database persistence
webhook ingestion in phase one
Reason:

Your stated goal is to learn blockchain reads and how to fetch data from Alchemy.
Those tools add complexity before you have finished the core explorer.
For a bootcamp assignment, clarity matters more than completeness.
Recommended Methods by Feature

For latest block and recent blocks:

Start with alchemy.core.getBlockNumber()
Then fetch recent blocks individually or by block number
Reason: this teaches the block model clearly
For block details:

Use alchemy.core.getBlock()
Use alchemy.core.getBlockWithTransactions() when you need tx objects
Reason: block page is the natural drill-down step
For transaction details:

Use transaction fetch plus receipt fetch
Reason: transaction core data and mined status are often split
For account balance:

Use alchemy.core.getBalance(address)
Reason: this is the simplest and most fundamental account query
For transfers received this year:

Use Alchemy asset transfer methods with filters
Reason: this is more meaningful than trying to reconstruct transfer history manually from raw blocks
For NFT metadata:

Use Alchemy NFT methods
Reason: this is exactly what the specialized SDK is good at
For “did a pending transaction get mined?”

Start with polling by tx hash
Reason: simpler than WebSocket, easier to understand, good for learning
Later, if you want, add WebSocket subscriptions as an enhancement
What to defer

WebSocket methods

Good as a second phase for realtime updates
Not required for the first clean version
Notify Webhooks

Better if you later add a backend
Not ideal for a frontend-only learning app
Transact API

Not necessary for a block explorer
Better suited to sending transactions, wallet actions, or gas abstraction workflows
Floor price

Nice extra if the SDK makes it easy
Not core to a block explorer assignment
Suggested build sequence

App shell and router
Home page with latest block number and recent blocks
Block details page with transaction list
Transaction details page
Account page with balance lookup
Transfers for address this year
NFT metadata lookup
Optional realtime status improvements
This order is deliberate:

each feature builds on concepts from the last one
it starts with the core chain entities: blocks, txs, accounts
it saves specialized APIs for after the core explorer works
One important design decision

I would keep the app frontend-only for now even though exposing the API key in a real app is not ideal.

Why:

your README already assumes that model
it is acceptable for a bootcamp learning exercise
adding a backend now would distract from the blockchain concepts
If you want, the next step should be one of these two:

I draft the exact data flow for each page, including which Alchemy methods each page should call.
I review your current App.js and tell you how to refactor it into the first route and component set, without changing code yet.